    <DataNode elementId="Phytoceramides" textLabel="a phytoceramide" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="31998" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">|FRAME: Ceramides &quot;A ceramide&quot;| is a lipid molecule that is composed of the sphingoid base |FRAME: SPHINGOSINE| and |FRAME: Fatty-Acids|. |FRAME: CPD3DJ-82 &quot;A dihydroceramide&quot;| is composed of the sphingoid base |FRAME: D-erythro-Shinganines sphinganine| (where the double bond in the sphingosine tail has been reduced) and a fatty acid. |FRAME: Phytoceramides &quot;A phytoceramide&quot;| is composed of the sphingoid base |FRAME: Phytosphingosines phytosphingosine| (a |FRAME: D-erythro-Shinganines sphinganine| that has been hydroxylated at position C4) and |FRAME: Very-long-chain-fatty-acids|, usually |FRAME: CPD-7832| or |FRAME: TETRACOSANOATE|. |FRAME: Phytoceramides &quot;Phytoceramides&quot;| are known to be produced by plants, yeast and mammals.</Comment>
